"""THE prediction artifact the manuscript reads from. One file, generated by gated code.

Everything the paper claims about an unmeasured compound must come from here. The reason this
script exists at all is that the eight predictions the project had been quoting lived only in a
scratchpad, while the one prediction file committed to the repo
(NOVEL_HALF_HEUSLER_PREDICTIONS.csv) issued four DIFFERENT compounds -- including ZrGaCu, HfGaCu
and HfInCu, which had already been refused three times for being hexagonal, and using stale
constants c=0.480 p=0.350 that contradict the calibration used everywhere else.

THE METHOD. For a compound that already has a published Boltzmann-transport calculation, no
machine learning is used or needed -- the calibrated calculation is measurably better than the ML
model (33.8% vs 42.1% median error, chemistry held out):

    kappa_measured = kappa_BTE x min(c (T/300)^p, 1)

(c, p) come from shared_constant.shared_cp(): fitted every run on the published calculation /
measurement pairs (PREREG_shared_constant_on_calculations.md; until 2026-10-05 they were refitted on
the ML model's blind-test predictions). They move with the reference set, so the value is written
into every output row and must be quoted with its provenance.

ONE GLOBAL CALIBRATION, NOT PER-FAMILY. The (Y,Z) families demonstrably differ in their raw
BTE/measured ratio (Sb-Pd 3.45, Ni-Sb 1.69, Sb-Pt 1.43 against a pooled 1.81), and an adversarial
review argued each prediction family therefore needs its own factor. Tested nested in
family_cal_nested.py: leave-one-compound-out 35.8% -> 35.3% (p=0.09, better on only 22 of 51), and
under the standard leave-one-chemistry-out protocol the family factor is WORSE, 33.7% -> 35.3%.
Rejected. The families differ in sample; that difference does not transfer.

GATES, each from a real failure:
  VEC == 18            below/above it the compound is metallic and lattice kappa is not the story
  full BTE label       43% of tier-1 half-Heusler formulae carry a semi-empirical Slack label
  no actinide/radio    no 5f element appears anywhere in training
  proven (Y,Z) class   Bi-Pd is excluded outright -- 0% within 2x
  CUBIC ON RECORD      new here; a spacegroup 216/225 entry from a non-Alexandria source
  POLYMORPH FLAGGED    new here; a compound also recorded hexagonal cannot be issued as though
                       the cubic phase is the one that forms
  T-BASIS DECLARED     new here; a compound with a single 300 K BTE point cannot support a
                       200-1000 K curve, and the row says so rather than implying one
  IN-RANGE OF FAMILY   new here; see below

THE MASS-TREND RULE WAS WRONG AND IS NOT USED. The project had been flagging LaSbPt and GdSbPt as
"breaking the mass trend of their series" -- La being the lightest rare earth yet getting the
lowest kappa. Tested in scratchpad/trend_gate.py against the measured data: there is no mass trend
to break. Across six families with >=4 measured members the median Spearman(X-site mass, kappa) is
+0.200, the sign is inconsistent, and in Sb-Pt it is +0.949 (p=0.051) -- heavier gives HIGHER
kappa, the opposite of the supposed rule. Screening on it would have been an invented rule.

What replaces it is a claim that can actually be checked: a prediction landing OUTSIDE the range
its own family's measurements span is an extrapolation, and is flagged as one with the range
quoted. That catches the same two compounds for a reason that survives inspection, and it catches
ScSbPt as well, which the mass rule missed.
"""
from __future__ import annotations
import os as _rel_os, sys as _rel_sys; _rel_sys.path[1:1] = [_rel_os.path.join(_rel_os.path.dirname(_rel_os.path.abspath(__file__)), "..", _d) for _d in ("analysis", "corpus", "checks", "paper", "")]  # release layout: see make_release.patch_release_paths

